military investigators are trying to  learn why c-130 cargo plane being flown  to retirement suddenly just plunged to  the ground  Wednesday's crash killed nine members of  the Puerto Rico National Guard this is  the latest in a string of military  aircraft accidents David Martin is at  the Pentagon with more on this story  David good morning good morning the  investigation into what caused  Wednesday's crash is just beginning  but it's hard not to think that planes  aids played a role that particular  aircraft was built in 1965 and was one  of the oldest in the airforce the final  flight of this c-130 was never supposed  to end like this a dive into the ground  and a fireball all those aboard never  had a chance we have confirmed that nine  Airmen in the Puerto Rico Air National  Guard were aboard the aircraft the plane  had just taken off from Savannah Georgia  bound for Arizona where it was to be  retired in an aircraft boneyard this  after a career which included ferrying  supplies to survivors of some of the  Caribbeans worst hurricanes it was a  tragedy for the island of Puerto Rico  which declared nine days of mourning for  the nine victims for the Pentagon it was  the latest in a string of deadly  accidents which have claimed 25 lives in  less than two months and left leaders  like the Commandant of the Marine Corps  searching for answers there's not one  single thing that's the you can't say  it's because of this an analysis of  accident data obtained by Military Times  found a nearly 40% increase in accidents  since 2013  that's when congressional spending  limits known as sequestration went into  effect and by one estimate cost the  Pentagon 147 billion dollars over five  years  that forced cuts in training and  maintenance at the same time aircraft  were in increasingly high demand for  operations like the bombing campaign  against Isis sequestration has ended for  now but chief pentagon spokesperson Dana  White says there are no quick fixes when  it comes to aircraft safety you can't  then you can't buy the training hours  back the fatal crashes get the public  attention but the rise in accidents was  due to relatively minor mishaps in which  no one was killed but the aircraft have  to be taken out of service for repairs  which means fewer planes and fewer  flying hours for pilots who start to  lose their skills  John David things is the human end of  all these sometimes abstract  conversations we have about budget
 

